---

Purpose

This document defines the high-level architecture of Ohira, the responsibilities of each subsystem, and how data flows from a software project into a structured knowledge model that can be consumed by developers, APIs, dashboards, and AI systems.

The architecture emphasizes:

Extensibility

Performance

Plugin-first design

AI compatibility

Framework independence



---

Architectural Principles

1. Local-First

By default, Ohira analyzes projects locally. Source code and project data remain on the developer's machine unless explicitly shared.


---

2. Plugin-Driven

The core engine contains no framework-specific logic. Support for technologies like Next.js, Prisma, Docker, and Git is provided through plugins.


---

3. AI-Native

Every component produces structured outputs that are easy for AI assistants to consume.


---

4. Knowledge-Centric

Ohira transforms files into a graph of meaningful entities and relationships rather than treating the project as a collection of directories.


